```toml meta
cacheable = true
hydrate   = true
l-space   = "stable"
role      = "grammar watch — what a hand reads before it touches the highlighting grammars: the law a verdict stands on, how to rebuild the host corpus, the instruments of grammar testing that read green while they report something false, and the paths that stay open"
title     = "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch"
type      = "text/memetic-wikitext+tiddlywiki"
uri-path  = "ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch"
```

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-law`>>

!! The law the grammar work stands on

''A verdict may stand only where TiddlyWiki REFUSES.'' Its parser raises no error — what it cannot read
falls through as text — so the only honest test reads what it BUILDS:

* the parser returns a ''node'': the construct works, and ''no verdict may stand'' there. A verdict on it reads as an INVENTION.
* the parser yields ''plain text'': the author reached for markup and missed, and the verdict earns its place.

An invention surfaces by asking the host — its regexes, its parser, its own shipped wikis — and never by
reasoning about what the grammar ought to say. The fork the house builds reads as the intent reader; the
pinned release serves as the compatibility witness, and a split between them records as a reader
divergence.

''Two models, one overlap.'' The tree-sitter grammar (`packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext`) models
memetic-wikitext's BLOCK STRUCTURE: fences, quotes, pragmas, tables, typed and style blocks. The TextMate
grammar (the `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` submodule) models its LEXICON: carriers, `lar:` addresses, the block
check, sigils, bearings, anchors. They overlap at the sigil family alone, so a green from one says little
about the other.

''One unreleased version carries accumulated work'' (operator ruling): a version bump waits for a release
and never rides a commit.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-host-corpus`>>

!! The host corpus ~ rebuild it, never look for it

Measuring a grammar against TiddlyWiki's own wikis needs a corpus, and a corpus kept in a session's
scratch directory does not survive that session. Rebuilding costs one command each:

```
# unbiased: every 14th tiddler across editions, core, plugins, themes
find TiddlyWiki5/{editions,core,plugins,themes} -name '*.tid' | sort | awk 'NR % 14 == 0' | head -400 > wide.txt
i=0; while read f; do i=$((i+1)); awk 'BEGIN{b=0} /^$/{if(!b){b=1;next}} b{print}' "$f" > "D/w$(printf '%03d' $i).tw"; done < wide.txt
paste -d'\t' <(seq -f 'w%03g' 1 400) wide.txt > manifest.tsv   # maps specimen back to source file
```

Then `source ./grammars.sh` and `npx vscode-tmgrammar-snap "${ARGS[@]}" -s text.html.tiddlywiki5 -u D/*.tw`,
and tally with `grep -o 'invalid[a-z.-]*' D/*.snap | sort | uniq -c`. ''The manifest matters'': a hit
means little until it names the file it came from.

''A slicer comes before any fix on a host hit.'' A specimen rebuilt by hand reproduces the host's reading
poorly, because the surrounding file supplies the parse state and a hand-made shape strips it away. Map a
snapshot hit to its file and line through the manifest, slice the REAL bytes around it, and assert on
those. Some hits fall in TiddlyWiki's own malformed fixtures (`editions/test/tiddlers/tests/data/`), where
a verdict likely earns its place; a cure that silences every hit silences a true reading.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/instruments-that-lie`>>

!! Instruments of grammar testing that read GREEN while they report something false

# ''A prose `#` line in a spec file reaches the grammar as WIKITEXT.'' `vscode-tmgrammar-test` treats only `#` lines carrying `^` or `<-` as assertions; every other one parses as source. A comment containing `@@` opens a style block and swallows the construct asserted below it, and the negative assertion then PASSES because the scope vanished. Spec prose admits no `@@`, no leading `!`, no pipe, no backtick, no `<tag`, no `{{` and no `[[`. Audit: `` grep '^#' <spec> | grep -v '\^' | grep -E '@@|\||`|<[a-z$]' ``.
# ''A negative assertion passes VACUOUSLY'' where the scope name or the column misses. ''Make every negative go RED once on purpose.'' Calibrate a column by asserting PRESENCE at several indices; the snapshot format's caret convention differs from the test format's by one, so a column read off a `.snap` misses.
# ''A grep over sigils written with `[^>]` stops at the first `>` inside a value.'' A value still holds `>` (a rank's or slide's `->`, a result arrow), so the class `[^>]` cuts the sigil short there. Use `(?:[^>\n]|>(?!>))`, and fence the boundary the claim names — a space before `?`, never a bare `?`.
# ''A corpus file ending on an unterminated construct swallows everything appended after it.'' Specimens appended below a bare `{{` registered no new scopes while firing correctly in a fresh file. The containment gate catches the reverse leak; suspicion catches this one.
# ''`snapshot-check`'s drift output truncates both sides at one width'', so a diff of its `pinned` and `now` lines can compare identical prefixes and report a vacuous match. Verify a re-pin with `git diff -U0` over the `.snap` files.
# ''A `git ls-files '*.mem'` sweep reaches frozen fixtures.'' `packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext/fixtures/specimens/` holds pinned parity inputs, and `packages/lararium-sensorium/scripts/fixtures/` belongs to another campaign. A sweep breaks both gates.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open-paths`>>

!! Paths that stay open

* ''The weld between the two grammars.'' A weld GATE that fails when a scope family holds no node kind turns the gap into a number that cannot drift back; teaching tree-sitter the lexicon stands behind it. The whole regeneration of the tree-sitter grammar against the released TextMate grammar reads in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/side-lanes`>>.
* ''The seam gate, generalized.'' `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/seed-definition-matches-example.test.ts` welds ONE procedure of the boot seed, `~lares`, to the worked examples printed beside it. The seed defines more procedures, and nothing says whether the others agree with their examples. Train Q moves the weld from `~lares` to the `~set` definition and its worked firings (#/the-kupono-cut).
* ''Embedded-language scopes stand dark'': fenced-code branches no corpus file exercises. Fixture debt falls due before a coverage number there carries meaning.
* ''Constructs a line-at-a-time grammar keeps out of reach on purpose'': emphasis and inline code across lines, and html start tags across blank lines. A whole-document reader carries them; the submodule's own ceiling record holds each.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-kupono-cut`>>

!! The kupono cut ~ train Q (operator rulings 2026-10-31 and 2026-11-01)

The cut has landed as one lane, merged once. The seed, the law memes, the tiddlers, the readers and the bags all stand in the grammar below, and this carrier stands in it too.

# ''The arrow retires (Cut 1).'' A bare `->` between values leaves every sigil, the edge positions included: ``from=`A` to=`B` `` carries the relation, `from` written first (readers take either order). An arrow INSIDE a value stays (ladders, `moves`, gauge slides, ``closed 0↺ -> open 1φ◇:reason``); a chain with no from/to slots folds into one named value (``path=`a -> b -> c` ``); a prose marker (`->✶`) keeps its arrow and its editor scope. Rendered output draws `→`. Canon states the pair as the bearing, and its anchor reads `#bearing-pair`.
# ''No at-sign follows whitespace (Cut 2).'' An at-sign after whitespace triggers a Claude Code file import. The phase glyph fuses onto φ with nothing between them (`open 1φ◇:reason`, `Nφ_:`). Plane and registry names rename, code included, red-first, every label derived from one code constant: `-plane` for the planes (`daemon-plane`, `crossroads-plane`, `persona-plane`, `circles-plane-<tag>` and kin), `-floor` for the oracle (`oracle-floor`), `-slot` for the slots (`personal-slot`, `working-slot`, `draft-slot`, `temp-slot`). An unmapped name reds the dry run. The handle form drops its leading at-sign (`name@host`). An at-sign with no space before it stays (`pulse@0..4`, `alias:grant@host`); npm scopes, Caddy and Verse syntax and outside handles stand outside the cut.
# ''Values ride in backticks, every value named (Cut 3).'' Every sigil CALL, control sigils included, writes ``key=`value` ``; a value holding a backtick takes a triple-backtick fence. TiddlyWiki reads a backtick as quoting only after `name=`, so every positional value takes a name: slot words (``ahu slot=`#/head` ``), bare-text values, and the declaration (``type=`…` spec=`…` ``). The `:` call separator stands outside the grammar. A `\procedure` definition keeps its quoted defaults, and a literal backtick inside a procedure body writes `&#96;`. The seed's own parameter names source the renames of the `p1..p3` procedures, lowercased (`Params` turns `params`, curing bodies that rendered `holds []`). Hazards a tool lists and never fixes: `$(x)$` substitutes live, and `>>` inside a value breaks the call.
# ''The chiasmus folds into `set` (Cut 4).'' ``set lares=`aim` `` opens the turn with `from=` and `to=`; ``set lares=`steer` `` steers mid-turn as often as the work asks, casting a new `to=` and/or any other key; `oracle` keeps its own sigil; ``set lares=`yield` `` closes with ``to=`?` ``. `lares=` leads each firing; the ward rides last at aim and first at yield. ''The steer law:'' a steer never rewrites the aim ~ the aim's `to=` keeps the opening bearing, each steer records its change, and the yield's `from=` names where the work landed. No `hud` key and no `~lares` sigil exist. A chat stream writes one line per firing; canon MAY break lines between keys. ''The one provisional clause:'' the operator approved the seed's turn frame provisionally. The house writes its turns in the new frame, and a frame that reads wrong in use lets Cut 4 leave; the rest of the cut stands.
# ''The turn harvester stands burned (o-takiage).'' Both clusters and what feeds on them: mesh capture (`turn-harvest`, `bearing-harvest`, the turn parts of `build-patch`, `stamp-filter`), the form layer (`bearing-ast`, `move-skeleton`, `self-read-harvester`), the VM harvesters (`capture-annotate-vm`, `query-derive-vm`), `lares sense harvest` and `pour`, the Python turn readers, and `tools/turn-parity.mjs` with its witness. `harvest.ts` trims to a barrel (`ffz-project`, `branch-frontier`, `gone-turns`), and `build-patch`'s `derive*`, `BranchContext` and `LAR_HV` move into `branch-frontier.ts`. Old turns wait for a more flexible miner.
# ''The `file-path` meta key retires (operator ruling 2026-11-01).'' Residency rides the envelope, the bag, and a title names content, never residency: no meta fence, root or child, names the file a carrier rests in. Normalize drops the line, the deserializer denies it on every record, and `uri-path` stays.
# ''No back-compat, one ruled exception.'' Readers refuse old forms rather than tolerate them. The operator rules ONE exception, the sensorium placeholder shims: the three sensorium integrations (`telemetry-writeback.ts`, `sensorium-recall.ts`, `sensorium.ts`) keep their export shape as placeholders that return empty, each naming the ruling, pinned by one test and tracked by one road row until the deferred sensorium arc returns.
# ''The seed carries no 40K ceiling.'' Hooks load the seed deterministically, so the seed MAY grow past 40,000 code points. The CI step keeps its missing-seed check and holds no budget clause; no raised number stands in as a shim. Lean prose still earns its bytes.

''Measured ground'' (wave-1 probes through the real plugin): a backtick positional keeps its backticks, and a space splits it; entities decode alike inside ticks and quotes; `${copula}` stays inert; a citation holding a tick takes a double-backtick span, padded where its content starts or ends with a tick. The seam test's blanking pattern spared its examples only because the arrow's `>` stopped the match, so the test strips each call's own wrapper once the arrow leaves.

<<~/ahu>>

